course: dictionary
lesson: 11408
title: Formal words and official approval
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words belong to official places, such as schools, newspapers and sport. They are called formal words. Accredited is one of them. You may not hear it at home, but you will see it on school letters, course leaflets and event passes.
explain: «The school is accredited.» The clues are official and trusted. An official body has checked the school and found it good enough, so parents can rely on it.
explain: «Only accredited reporters could go into the stadium.» Reporters with an accredited pass are officially allowed in. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Approved fits well, so accredited means officially approved.
fact: Accredited means officially approved or checked, and therefore trusted.
fact: A school, nursery, course or coach can be accredited when it meets a set standard.
fact: A person can be accredited to do a job or to be somewhere, such as an accredited reporter at an event.
fact: Accredited to someone means given credit for something, as in «The idea is accredited to Mia.»
fact: Accredit is the verb, meaning to officially approve someone or something, or to give credit for it.
exercise: «The nursery is accredited, so an official body has checked it.» What does accredited mean? | Painted in bright colours | *Officially approved as good enough | Very noisy | Closed for the holidays
exercise: Find and copy the one word that means officially approved in «Only accredited reporters could go into the stadium.» | stadium | could | *accredited | reporters
exercise: «The idea is accredited to Mia.» What does this mean? | Mia must pay for the idea | The idea is for sale | *Mia is given credit for the idea | The idea has been lost
test: Mum says, «Choose a course that is fully accredited.» What should she look for? | A course that is very short | A course with lots of games | A course with the cheapest price | *A course officially checked and approved
test: «Only accredited coaches can teach at the club.» Why are these coaches allowed to teach? | They are the tallest in the club | They shout the loudest | *They are officially approved to do the job | They have the newest kit
exam: «The sports club is accredited by an official body, so it is trusted.» What is the best meaning of accredited here? | Very old and broken | *Officially approved and trusted | Closed to new members | Painted green
